1
00:00:01,230 --> 00:00:05,950
Hello, my name is Yulong
Chen. This is my project.

2
00:00:06,210 --> 00:00:10,610
This project asks when voice
activity detection helps

3
00:00:10,610 --> 00:00:15,330
Wiener speech enhancement
update its noise estimate.

4
00:00:16,010 --> 00:00:20,110
The trade-off is simple: updating
can follow changing noise,

5
00:00:20,250 --> 00:00:24,030
but speech mistakenly
included in the estimate

6
00:00:24,030 --> 00:00:26,330
may also be suppressed.

7
00:00:30,750 --> 00:00:35,490
The shared two-step noise
reduction algorithm comes from

8
00:00:35,490 --> 00:00:40,570
an existing implementation,
credited in the report.

9
00:00:41,010 --> 00:00:45,230
My work is the controlled
adaptation, verification,

10
00:00:45,930 --> 00:00:48,190
experiments and analysis.

11
00:00:48,790 --> 00:00:52,290
Fixed estimation and both VAD settings

12
00:00:52,290 --> 00:00:55,690
share the same processing
and reconstruction.

13
00:00:55,830 --> 00:00:59,410
Only the noise update rule changes.

14
00:01:00,030 --> 00:01:02,770
Clean speech is used for evaluation,

15
00:01:02,770 --> 00:01:05,850
never as an input to the enhancer.

16
00:01:10,603 --> 00:01:16,343
The original VAD uses a 3 dB threshold

17
00:01:16,643 --> 00:01:18,683
and a five-frame hangover.

18
00:01:19,003 --> 00:01:23,503
I compared nine settings on
16 development conditions.

19
00:01:24,013 --> 00:01:27,133
The highest mean STOI

20
00:01:27,133 --> 00:01:30,613
came from a threshold of 1 dB

21
00:01:30,613 --> 00:01:33,793
and hangover of ten frames.

22
00:01:34,333 --> 00:01:37,413
This more cautious
setting was then frozen

23
00:01:37,413 --> 00:01:41,973
for 48 conditions using six sentences.

24
00:01:42,913 --> 00:01:46,193
The speakers overlap earlier material.

25
00:01:47,073 --> 00:01:52,333
These are repeated conditions,
not 48 independent speakers,

26
00:01:52,833 --> 00:01:55,673
and no retuning followed this test.

27
00:01:59,233 --> 00:02:04,603
This demonstration runs
the predetermined example:

28
00:02:04,883 --> 00:02:08,383
sentence sp02, street noise,

29
00:02:08,863 --> 00:02:11,703
at nominal five decibels.

30
00:02:12,103 --> 00:02:15,623
The program recomputes four outputs

31
00:02:15,623 --> 00:02:19,863
and verifies their scores
against saved results.

32
00:02:20,443 --> 00:02:24,263
The upper panels show this fresh run.

33
00:02:24,823 --> 00:02:29,023
SNR measures waveform
error against clean speech.

34
00:02:29,703 --> 00:02:33,163
STOI predicts intelligibility;

35
00:02:33,683 --> 00:02:37,083
it is not a percentage
of words understood.

36
00:02:37,883 --> 00:02:41,323
Here, processing improves SNR

37
00:02:41,323 --> 00:02:43,823
but reduces STOI.

38
00:02:44,203 --> 00:02:47,103
The selected setting is not best.

39
00:02:50,983 --> 00:02:56,383
The lower panels show stored
means over all 48 conditions,

40
00:02:56,723 --> 00:02:58,443
not a live rerun.

41
00:02:59,163 --> 00:03:03,403
Selected VAD improves mean STOI over

42
00:03:03,403 --> 00:03:08,043
original VAD by about 0.004.

43
00:03:09,143 --> 00:03:14,363
Fixed estimation still has
slightly higher mean STOI.

44
00:03:14,983 --> 00:03:19,323
The fraction of reference
speech-proxy frames

45
00:03:19,323 --> 00:03:25,643
used for updates falls
from about 16% to 3%.

46
00:03:25,643 --> 00:03:29,063
This suggests stronger speech protection,

47
00:03:29,363 --> 00:03:33,323
with fewer opportunities to track noise.

48
00:03:33,803 --> 00:03:37,083
The small effects are
checked sentence by sentence,

49
00:03:37,403 --> 00:03:40,543
rather than treating
repeated noise conditions

50
00:03:40,543 --> 00:03:42,983
as independent observations.

51
00:03:49,483 --> 00:03:52,523
To test that trade-off more directly,

52
00:03:52,963 --> 00:03:55,463
I added 36 matched mixtures.

53
00:03:55,983 --> 00:03:59,143
The speech and base
noise remain the same;

54
00:03:59,643 --> 00:04:02,263
only the noise level is held constant,

55
00:04:02,823 --> 00:04:06,863
raised six decibels or
lowered six decibels.

56
00:04:07,823 --> 00:04:09,383
Parameters stay frozen.

57
00:04:09,943 --> 00:04:14,383
After the rise, neither VAD
updates in the late interval:

58
00:04:14,383 --> 00:04:18,863
higher energy can keep the speech
gate closed to updates.

59
00:04:19,663 --> 00:04:24,243
After the fall, updates
resume and the selected method

60
00:04:24,243 --> 00:04:28,683
shows small advantages
over fixed estimation.

61
00:04:29,403 --> 00:04:33,443
These constructed,
previously used sentences are

62
00:04:33,443 --> 00:04:36,783
a mechanism diagnostic,

63
00:04:37,203 --> 00:04:41,123
not an independent generalisation test.

64
00:04:44,043 --> 00:04:47,513
My conclusion is that
careful noise updates

65
00:04:47,513 --> 00:04:49,733
reduce one type of error,

66
00:04:50,313 --> 00:04:54,093
but they do not solve noise tracking
in every situation.

67
00:04:54,893 --> 00:04:58,313
All main comparisons
use the same standard

68
00:04:58,313 --> 00:05:01,873
Python STOI implementation.

69
00:05:02,453 --> 00:05:05,893
Effects are small,
sentence outcomes vary,

70
00:05:05,893 --> 00:05:11,593
and subjective listening
quality was not evaluated.

71
00:05:12,373 --> 00:05:15,393
More independent speakers and longer,

72
00:05:16,713 --> 00:05:19,793
genuinely changing recordings are needed.

73
00:05:20,373 --> 00:05:21,233
Thank you.
